    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, primarily composed of phosphatidylcholine, acts as an emulsifier by positioning its hydrophilic phosphate head group at the aqueous phase while its lipophilic fatty acid tails anchor into the oil droplets. This orientation lowers the interfacial tension, effectively preventing the coalescence of oil and water droplets through steric hindrance and electrostatic repulsion. In high-acid environments, this stable interface helps maintain a homogeneous emulsion by resisting the charge screening that often causes phase separation in other ointments.",
